# Project Steward workflow

Read this document before working on the project. Project-specific rules in
AGENTS.md and explicit user instructions take precedence.

## Project records

- PROJECT.md describes the purpose, constraints, and success criteria.
- PLAN.md holds milestone goals and either detailed tasks or a backend overview.
- HANDOFF.md records current state and executable next steps.
- PROGRESS.md records changes; DECISIONS.md records decisions and their reasons.
- QUESTIONS.md, RISKS.md, and VERIFY.md track unknowns, risks, and validation.
- config.toml controls commit and session preferences. backend.json identifies
  the authoritative task backend.

Invalid known config values use safe defaults and are reported by
`project-steward doctor`; valid unrelated settings remain available.

All these files live in `.project-steward/`. Runtime files are local and ignored
by Git; durable records travel with the repository.

## Start a session

Read HANDOFF.md, then run `project-steward resume` when the CLI is available.
Recap the task, next step, blockers, open questions, Git state, and crash signals.
Without the CLI, read PROGRESS.md, PLAN.md, QUESTIONS.md, the selected backend,
and recent Git history. Label reconstructed state as inferred. Resume does not
rewrite committed project records. Treat an active local runtime marker as an
advisory note; use separate handoff, activity, dirty-work, commit, or
Git-operation evidence to decide whether recovery is needed.

## Record meaningful changes

When work starts, completes, becomes blocked, or changes direction, update the
relevant project records. Record validation results in VERIFY.md and PROGRESS.md.
Refresh HANDOFF.md's Now, In flight, Next steps, and Blockers when they change.
`project-steward checkpoint --note "..."` appends progress and refreshes metadata;
it does not write a substantive handoff body or query an external task backend.
Do not rewrite files when nothing material changed.

## Commit coherent work

Read `[git] commit_policy` in config.toml. New projects default to `auto`;
existing settings remain in force. Missing or invalid legacy settings use `ask`.

- `auto`: after a coherent task, fix, tested refactor, or milestone, run the
  relevant checks, review the diff and staged changes, then commit the related
  code, tests, task artifacts, and project records together without asking again.
- `ask`: prepare a concrete commit and ask before committing.
- `never`: do not commit or nudge for a commit.

Use Conventional Commits and explicit paths or hunks. For whole-file commits with
unrelated changes already staged, use `git commit --only -m "..." -- <paths>`
so those staged changes remain untouched. If a file mixes user and agent changes
and the intended hunks cannot be isolated safely, skip the automatic commit. Preserve unrelated and
pre-existing changes. If ownership is unclear or relevant checks fail, explain
why a code commit was skipped and keep the handoff accurate. Avoid empty or
timer-driven commits. Hooks only prompt for bookkeeping; they never commit.
`wrap --commit` is a stewardship-file convenience, not a complete feature commit.
Never push, force-push, or rewrite published history without explicit approval.

## Finish or hand off

Write HANDOFF.md for someone without the previous chat: completed work, unfinished
work, blockers, validation evidence, useful file paths, and concrete next steps.
Keep this level of detail with every task backend. Update the other records,
then run `project-steward wrap --summary "..."` when available and follow the
commit policy. A metadata-only checkpoint cannot replace an updated handoff body.

## Maintain instructions and hooks

Treat AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md as user-owned. Edit only PROJECT-STEWARD managed
blocks, show their diff first, and record approved changes in DECISIONS.md.
Keep CLAUDE.md a thin @AGENTS.md adapter. Preserve custom workflow guidance.

Initialization includes project-local Codex hooks unless --no-codex-hooks is
selected. The CLI must be installed on PATH for those hooks. Codex still requires
project trust and hook review through `/hooks`; installed files alone do not prove
activation. The session protocol above also works without hooks or installed skills.
